{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-5436",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "The formula for the $n$th term is given by  \n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={a}_{1}+(n-1)d,\n$$\n\nso we need to figure out the first term ${a}_{1}$ and the common difference $d.$\n\nTo find the common difference, we can find the total difference between the terms ${a}_{19}=99$ and ${a}_{4}=-6$ and divide it by the number of jumps, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\frac{{a}_{19}-{a}_{4}}{19-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{99-(-6)}{19-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{105}{15} \\\\  & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can use one of our known values, say, ${a}_{4}=-6,$ to determine the first term of the sequence. Since ${a}_{4}$ is $3$ jumps after ${a}_{1},$ we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{4} & ={a}_{1}+3d \\\\ -6 & ={a}_{1}+3\\cdot 7 \\\\ -6 & ={a}_{1}+21 \\\\ {a}_{1} & =-27\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the formula for the $n$th term is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =-27+(n-1)\\cdot 7 \\\\  & =7n-34\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can find the $11$th term by substituting $n=11$ into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & =7(11)-34 \\\\  & =77-34 \\\\  & =43\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
